  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Colon cancer is a treatable, often curable malignancy developing from polyps in the colon or rectum, with high survival rates if caught early (91% for stages I/II). It primarily affects adults over 45, though incidence is rising in younger people, with key symptoms including persistent bowel habit changes, rectal bleeding, abdominal pain, and unexplained weight loss. Early Signs and SymptomsOften, early-stage colon cancer is asymptomatic. When symptoms occur, they may include: Changes in bowel habits: Diarrhea, constipation, or narrowing of the stool that does not go away. Rectal bleeding: Blood in the stool or bright red blood from the rectum. Abdominal discomfort: Frequent gas pains, bloating, fullness, or cramps. Unexplained weight loss: Losing weight without trying. Fatigue/Weakness: Feeling consistently tired. Iron-deficiency anemia: A new-onset of low iron found in blood tests. Risk FactorsKey factors increasing the likelihood of developing colon cancer include: Age: Risk increases with age, particularly over 50, but cases are rising in those under 50. Personal/Family History: A history of colorectal polyps, colorectal cancer, or inflammatory bowel diseases like ulcerative colitis or Crohn's disease. Lifestyle Factors: Diets high in red or processed meats, sedentary lifestyle, obesity, smoking, and heavy alcohol use. Genetics: Inherited syndromes, such as Lynch syndrome, increase risk. Stages of Colon CancerColon cancer is classified using the TNM staging system, which dictates treatment plans: Stage 0 (Carcinoma in situ): Very early cancer on the innermost layer of the colon. Stage I/II: Cancer has grown deeper into the wall of the colon but has not spread to lymph nodes or other organs. Stage III: Cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes. Stage IV (Metastatic): Cancer has spread to other parts of the body, such as the liver or lungs. Prognosis and Prevention Survival: The five-year survival rate is approximately 91% for stages I and II, but drops to 13% for Stage IV. Screening: Regular screenings (e.g., colonoscopies) starting at age 45 can detect and remove precancerous polyps, making it highly preventable. Treatment: Primary treatment is often surgery, sometimes combined with chemotherapy, radiation, or targeted therapies.",
